WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.027 --> 00:00:09.110
SPEAKER: How does Google
design a data center that

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.630
can support millions
of connected end points

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.030
and requests simultaneously?

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:18.020
At Google, it took 18 years of
work downloading an entire web

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.670
and index.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.490
Back in the early days,
we ran on a limited number

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.550
of high-end traditional
enterprise servers and storage

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.160
in a shared facility,
leading to what's

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.890
now one of the world's
largest computing systems

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.160
made up of multi-acre
sized data centers.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.520
A data center consists of
compute, storage, networking,

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.560
and power and cooling
infrastructure

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.730
in order to handle demand.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.850
Think of all the parts
like building blocks

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.360
that talk to each other.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.470
Red blocks are server halls
with compute and networking.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:49.970
Green are power supply.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.722
And blue are cooling
infrastructure.

00:00:52.722 --> 00:00:53.930
Let's start with red blocks--

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.280
server rooms.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.290
Chips comprise
processing and memory

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.890
in a server, which also
includes hard drives or storage.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.260
These servers are
consolidated into racks

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.630
and positioned into rows.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.270
Thanks to Moore's Law, we
can process more information

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.760
than ever before in a
smaller computing unit.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.390
Today, that means a single
data center building

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.730
with thousands of
server racks holding

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.430
thousands of gigabytes of data.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:22.130
All of this is possible
through our green blocks--

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.450
power supply.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.700
An electric system
feeds into the building

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.090
and provides a steady stream of
electrons flowing to servers.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.670
And just in case our
electric system goes out,

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:35.150
we employ a backup energy source
to provide redundant power

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.070
supplies to servers.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.440
A mechanical system also
feeds into the building

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.200
to help remove heat from
hard working servers.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.140
These high-density servers
generate a lot of heat.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.110
But by continuously raising
the operating temperature

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:51.230
in our data centers as
computing technology allows,

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.180
we use less energy.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.030
And that leads us to
our next crucial piece--

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.160
our blue blocks-- cooling.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.710
Hot/cold aisle is a
server aisle layout

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.960
that increases the
effectiveness of the cooling

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.360
system by managing airflow.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.610
Server racks are
lined up with cold air

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:11.070
facing one way and hot
exhaust facing the other.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.850
We distribute chilled water
through an array of pipes

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.640
over to fan coil units
that service the racks

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.560
and help disperse heat.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.600
As the water is
warmed by the servers,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.700
it's moved through
an array of pipes,

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.570
then to a cooling tower
for evaporative cooling.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:29.810
Using blocks as a framework,
designing a data center

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.220
becomes easier.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:34.610
The real challenge-- massive
scale and the core principles

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.470
behind running a data center.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.540
More on that next on
Discovering Data Centers.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.190
If you want to learn more, check
out google.com/datacenters.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

